  "scope": "T and R sum the native transmitted and reflected modal powers for the matched axial input. Each relative change uses the lower-layer fraction as its baseline. The absolute increment ratio divides the magnitude of the two-to-three-layer change by that of the one-to-two-layer change. Body layers and compatible port discretization change together at fixed physical thickness.",
  "limits": "Finite saved calculations at one frequency with the original physical sections and end planes. Normalized coordinates are used only for displacement matching; T and R are fractions of native incident power. This single-drive comparison leaves all-channel and complex-response failures in force. Weak reflection and relative changes near zero remain sensitive to discretization. Spatial and frequency convergence, a reflection floor, and continuous branch identity remain unresolved. The trend supplies neither a continuum error bound nor a three-layer thickness contrast."
